" (https://www.bo.infn.it/herab/people/zoccoli/didattica/esperimenti_fisica/E1.1_Scattering_Geiger%20%26%20Marsden%20Study%20Alpha%20Particle%20Scattering.pdf)\nciteturn52view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 4; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.bo.infn.it/herab/people/zoccoli/didattica/esperimenti_fisica/E1.1_Scattering_Geiger%20%26%20Marsden%20Study%20Alpha%20Particle%20Scattering.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Redirected to URL: https://www.bo.infn.it/herab/people/zoccoli/didattica/esperimenti_fisica/E1.1_Scattering_Geiger%20&%20Marsden%20Study%20Alpha%20Particle%20Scattering.pdf; Total lines: 141\nL0@P0: URL: https://www.bo.infn.it/herab/people/zoccoli/didattica/esperimenti_fisica/E1.1_Scattering_Geiger%20&%20Marsden%20Study%20Alpha%20Particle%20Scattering.pdf\nL1@P0: On a Diffuse Reflection of the α-Particles\nL2@P0: Proc. Roy. Soc. 1909 A\nL3@P0: vol. 82, p. 495-500\nL4@P0: By H. GEIGER, Ph.D., John Harling Fellow, and E. MARSDEN, Hatfield Scholar, University of Manchester.\nL5@P0: (Communicated by Prof. E. Rutherford, F.R.S. Received May 19, -- Read June 17, 1909.)\nL6@P0: 495\nL7@P0: When β-particles fall on a plate, a strong radiation emerges from the same side of the plate as that on which the\nL8@P0: β-particles fall. This radiation is regarded by many observers as a secondary radiation, but more recent\nL9@P0: experiments seem to show that it consists mainly of primary β-particles, which have been scattered inside the\nL10@P0: material to such an extent that they emerge again at the same side of the plate.* For α-particles a similar effect\nL11@P0: has not previously been observed, and is perhaps not to be expected on account of the relatively small scattering\nL12@P0: which α-particles suffer in penetrating matter.**\nL13@P0: In the following experiments, however, conclusive evidence was found of the existence of a diffuse reflection of\nL14@P0: the α-particles. A small fraction of the α-particles falling upon a metal plate have their directions changed to\nL15@P0: such an extent that they emerge again at the side of incidence. To form an idea of the way in which this effect\nL16@P0: takes place, the following three points were investigated:\nL17@P0: (I) The relative amount of reflection from different metals.\nL18@P0: (II) The relative amount of reflection from a metal of varying thickness.\nL19@P0: (III) The fraction of the incident α-particles which are reflected.\nL20@P0: * See Schmidt, 'Jahrbuch der Radioaktivitat und Electronik,' vol. 5, p. 471, 1908.\nL21@P0: ** Rutherford, 'Phil. Mag.,' vol. 12, p. 143, 1906; H. Geiger, 'Roy. Soc. Proc.,' A, vol. 81, p. 174,1908.\nL22@P0: 496\nL23@P0: For the observation of the reflected particles the scintillation method was used in all experiments. With regard to\nL24@P0: the details of the method we refer to the papers of Regener* and of Rutherford and Geiger.**\nL25@P0: On account of the fact that the amount of reflection is very small, it was necessary to use a very intense source of\nL26@P0: α-rays. A tube was employed similar to that which has been proved to be a suitable source in the scattering\nL27@P0: experiments of one of us.*** This source consisted of a glass tube AB (fig. 1), drawn down conically and filled\nL28@P0: with radium emanation, the end B of the tube being closed airtight by\nL29@P0: means of a mica window. The thickness of the mica was equivalent to\nL30@P0: about 1 cm. of air, so that the α-particles could easily pass through it.\nL31@P0: Since it is of importance that the gas pressure inside this tube should be\nL32@P0: as low as possible, the emanation was purified according to the\nL33@P0: methods developed by Prof. Rutherford.**** The tube contained an\nL34@P0: amount of emanation equivalent to about 20 milligrammes RaBr2 at a\nL35@P0: pressure of a few centimetres. The number of α-particles expelled per\nL36@P0: second through the window was, therefore, very great, and, on account\nL37@P0: of the small pressure inside the tube, the different ranges of the α\u0002particles from the three products (i.e. emanation, RaA, and RaC) were\nL38@P0: sharply defined.\nL39@P0: The zinc sulphide screen S (fig. 1) was fixed behind the lead plate P, in\nL40@P0: such a position that no α-particles could strike it directly. When a\nL41@P0-1: reflector was placed in the position RR at about 1 cm. from the end ofthe tube, scintillations were at once observed. At the same time the screen brightened up appreciably on account\nL42@P1: of the reflected β-particles.\nL43@P1: By means of a low power microscope, the number of scintillations per minute on a definite square millimetre of\nL44@P1: the screen was counted for reflectors of different materials. Care was taken that the different reflectors were\nL45@P1: always placed in exactly the same position.\nL46@P1: It is, of course, to be expected that the number of α-particles reflected from the plate would be different in\nL47@P1: different directions, and would also depend on the angle of incidence. In our arrangement, however, no\nL48@P1: appreciable difference was found for different angles. This is due to the fact that,\nL49@P1: * 'Verh. d. D. Phys. Ges.,' vol. 10, p. 78,1908.\nL50@P1: ** 'Roy. Soc. Proc.,' A, vol. 81, p. 141, 1908.\nL51@P1: *** Geiger, 'Roy. Soc. Proc.,' A, vol. 81, p. 174, 1908.\nL52@P1: **** 'Phil. Mag.,' August, p. 300, 1908.\nL53@P1: 497\nL54@P1: owing to the necessity of having the tube very near to the reflector, the angle of incidence varied very much. An\nL55@P1: investigation of the variation of the effect with the angles of incidence and emergence would necessitate a.\nL56@P1: parallel and very intense source of homogeneous α-rays, which can, however, not easily be realised.\nL57@P1: In the following table the number of scintillations observed per minute are given in column 3; in column 4 the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nci